  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/74e3e8f0f4f221f016f0894fb2720f98075c9769f1c4fb3cad9aac9042e05cb6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/512d556f62388e66e7fa0ecaf2be7f73cf973e07d641fd37e901d354a11257b1.png)\n\nThe point $(-5,-7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-5,-3)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-3=4.$",
